\section{Cold coupon}
For the first coupon, let $V$ be its terminal voltage, $I$ the current entering its marked positive terminal, and $R$ its resistance. We use the passive sign convention. The bench relation is
\[ V = R I . \]

\section{Heated coupon}
The notation is restarted for the second coupon: here $V$ is its terminal voltage, $I$ is the current entering its marked positive terminal, and $R$ is its hot resistance. We again use the passive sign convention. Its measured relation is
\[ V = R I . \]

\section{Index sets}
Let $A$ and $B$ be finite sets of sensor labels. Their overlap is $A\cap B$.

\section{Alarm experiment}
We now reuse the letters locally. In one probability space, $A$ is the event that the smoke channel fires and $B$ is the event that the heat channel fires. The conditioning event has positive probability, $P(B)>0$. Thus the conditional probability is
\[ P(A\mid B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)} . \]
